<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iskandar Beg Munshi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1561–<a href="/years/1634">1634</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iskandar Beg Munshi was an Iranian court scribe and chronicler, author of the Tarikh-e Alam-ara-ye Abbasi, &quot;The world-adorning history of Abbas&quot;, a history of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> and above all of the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> (r. 1588–1629). Born in 1561 or 1562 into a Turkoman clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>, the militant Shia group that had helped the Safavids to power, he joined the bureaucracy rather than the military elite, training as a pupil scribe of Mirza Ata-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> Isfahani under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Shah Tahmasp I</a>. His chronicle, influenced by the Mughal Akbarnama, is considered the most significant work of Iranian historiography on Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. He died in 1633 or 1634.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iskandar-beg-munshi.jpg" alt="Iskandar Beg Munshi" width="330" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: loc.gov · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuscript_of_the_Kitāb-i_Tārīkh-i_ʻĀlamʹāra_by_Eskandar_Beg_Monshi,_Qajar_Iran,_dated_July_1812.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gifts of vaqf land turned many clerical families into landowners, a new and significant group in the words of the chronicler <b>Iskandar Munshi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/safavid-iran">Social Hierarchy of Safavid Iran</a></li></ul></section>
